WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.702
Now follow the conversion one step further.

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:09.597
The tokenizer first turns the sentence into model tokens and then maps those tokens to integer IDs.

00:00:09.597 --> 00:00:17.597
The encoded dictionary contains input_ids, and in many cases it will also contain fields such as

00:00:17.597 --> 00:00:20.937
attention_mask or token_type_ids depending on the model.

00:00:20.937 --> 00:00:26.557
Printing the IDs lets us see the exact numeric representation passed into the model.

00:00:26.557 --> 00:00:32.172
Converting those IDs back to token labels makes the mapping easier to interpret.

00:00:32.172 --> 00:00:38.742
Notice the special tokens at the boundaries and any subword markers inside uncommon words.

00:00:38.742 --> 00:00:44.592
A useful experiment is to change just one word and compare the ID sequence.

00:00:44.592 --> 00:00:49.572
Another is to encode the same text with a different model’s tokenizer.

00:00:49.572 --> 00:00:54.982
The numbers will usually change because each tokenizer has its own vocabulary.

00:00:54.982 --> 00:00:59.680
That is another reason the tokenizer and pretrained checkpoint must stay together.
